==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)ker- (cut)}}
From {{bor|en|la|corium|t=leather}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|s|coria}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The layer of [[skin]] between the [[epidermis]] and the [[subcutaneous]] [[tissue]]s; the [[dermis]].
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The deep layer of [[mucous membrane]]s beneath the [[epithelium]].
# {{lb|en|historical}} [[armour|Armour]] made of [[leather]], particularly that used by the [[Roman]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1825|author=Thomas Dudley Fosbroke|title=Encyclopaedia of antiquities, and elements of archaeology, classical and mediæval|passage=Passing by the '''''Corium''' Bubulum'' of the Classical Ancients, we see in an old charter, dated 1036, "''Stallus Sutoris Vaccæ''," ''i. e.'' the stall of a shoe-maker who used cow-skin.}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|layer of skin between the epidermis and the subcutaneous tissues}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|verinahka}}, {{t|fi|corium}}
* German: {{t|de|Corium|n}}
* Romanian: {{t+|ro|corium|n}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|dermis|f}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{suffix|en|core|ium}}. Apparently coined in 1974 by Martin Peehs, a German nuclear engineer.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|nuclear physics}} A [[lavalike]] mixture of [[fissile]] material created in a [[nuclear reactor]]'s core during a [[nuclear]] [[meltdown]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Martin Peehs |year=1974 |title=Investigations of molten "'''corium'''" phases |journal=Thermodynamics of Nuclear Materials |volume=1 |publisher=International Atomic Agency IAEA-SM-190/10 |location=Vienna }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1975 |month=21 October |title=Hearings on the Nuclear Initiative: Technological Concerns in Nuclear Reactor Safety |publisher=California Legislature Assembly Committee on Resources, Land Use, and Energy |location=Sacramento (CA) |passage=Mexico was given $100,000 to do a series of experiments in which small amounts (10 to 20 grams) of stainless steel and '''corium''' (a mixture of 55 percent iron, 10 percent zirconium, and 35 percent uranium dioxide) will be melted and then flooded with water in an arc-melting chamber.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1991 |title=Numerical Modeling of '''Corium'''/Steel Structure Interaction with Downfacing Boiling Boundary Conditions
|url=https://pennstate.pure.elsevier.com/en/publications/numerical-modeling-of-coriumsteel-structure-interaction-with-down |author=Sheau-Wen Shiah; Fan-bill B. Cheung; Dae-Ho Cho; Michael J. Tan; Louis Baker Jr. |page=31 |journal=Numerical Modelling of Basic Heat Transfer Phenomena in Nuclear Systems, presented at the 28th National Heat Transfer Conference, Minneapolis, Minnesota, July 28-31, 1991 |editors=Chung, Franklin; Hochreiter, Larry E. |publisher=American Society of Mechanical Engineers |location=New York (NY) |passage=Previous studies of the thermal behavior of '''corium''' in a degraded nuclear reactor have focussed primarily on the process of heat transfer within the '''corium'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2009 |author=Wei Wei; Xing-rong Cao |title=The Simulation of '''Corium''' Dispersion in Direct Containment Heating Accidents |journal=Zero Carbon Energy Kyoto 2009 }}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2011 |author=Pascal Piluso |journal=Nuclear Safety in Light Water Reactors: Severe Accident Phenomenology |editor=Bal Raj Seghal |ISBN=978-0-12-388446-6 |publisher=Academic Press |location=Waltham (MA) |page=664 |title=Appendix 1: '''Corium''' Thermodynamics and Thermophysics, 2. '''Corium''' Thermophysics |passage=The materials of the nuclear reactor—nuclear oxide fuel and fission products, cladding, metallic alloys, moderator, absorbers, structural materials, coolants, concrete, and so on, could melt to form complex, multiphases, and aggressive mixtures known by the general term '''''corium'''''. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019 |month=October |title=INIS [International Nuclear Information System] Thesaurus, Multilingual version, IAEA-INIS Reference Series IAEA-INIS-01 (2019/10) |publisher=IAEA [International Atomic Energy Agency] |location=Vienna |ISSN=1684-095X |page=264 |url=https://www.iaea.org/sites/default/files/19/10/multi-2019-10.pdf |passage='''CORIUM''': INIS: 1977-10-17; ETDE: 1977-06-02 Molten mixture of fuel, cladding and other core structural material resulting from a meltdown accident.}}

